`errorHandler({ dumpExceptions: true, showStack: true })` is a configuration object used by Express.js, a popular Node.js web framework, to customize its error handling behavior.

Here's a breakdown of the properties:

1. **`dumpExceptions`**: When set to `true`, Express will dump the exception object (i.e., the error object) to the console. This can be helpful for debugging purposes.
2. **`showStack`**: When set to `true`, Express will display the full stack trace of the error, including the line numbers and file names. This can be useful for identifying the source of the error.

By setting both `dumpExceptions` and `showStack` to `true`, you're telling Express to:

* Print the details of the exception object to the console
* Include the full stack trace with line numbers and file names

This configuration is useful for development environments where you want to quickly identify and diagnose errors. In production environments, you may want to set `dumpExceptions` and `showStack` to `false` to prevent sensitive error information from being exposed to users.

To set up this error handler in an Express.js app, you can do something like this:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(express.errorHandler({ dumpExceptions: true, showStack: true }));

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port 3000');
});
```
Keep in mind that error handling is an important aspect of building robust and reliable web applications. By configuring your error handler wisely, you can provide a better experience for your users and improve the overall quality of your app.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

